{"records":[{"id":"n5mjjfcdc2","title":"Cell-specific targeting by Clostridium perfringens β-toxin unraveled: the role of CD31 as the toxin receptor.","description":"Data set for publication in Cell Host Microbe, Bruggisser et al: Cell-specific targeting by Clostridium perfringens β-toxin unraveled: the role of CD31 as the toxin receptor.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-04T07:00:58.559Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5mjjfcdc2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5mjjfcdc2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Horst","last_name":"Posthaus","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julia","last_name":"Bruggisser","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Receptor","Pore Forming Protein","Clostridium"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x55r5h6j4g","title":"Human-specific staphylococcal virulence factors enhance pathogenicity in a humanised zebrafish C5a receptor model.","description":"Provided are the raw data for \"Humanisation of the zebrafish C5a receptor confers targeting by human-specific staphylococcal virulence factors\", available at https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.02.18.955021v2\nThis mostly includes raw imaging data that we used to make the figures, in addition to some Prism files we used for data processing.\n\nBelow is a copy of the abstract for this work.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus infects approximately 30% of the human population and causes a spectrum of pathologies ranging from mild skin infections to life-threatening invasive diseases. The strict host specificity of its virulence factors has severely limited the accuracy of in vivo models for the development of vaccines and therapeutics. To resolve this, we generated a humanised zebrafish model and determined that neutrophil-specific expression of the human C5a receptor conferred susceptibility to the S. aureus toxins PVL and HlgCB, leading to reduced neutrophil numbers at the site of infection and increased infection-associated mortality as a direct result of the interaction between S. aureus and the receptor. These results show that humanised zebrafish provide a valuable platform to study the contribution of human-specific S. aureus virulence factors to infection in vivo that could facilitate the development of novel therapeutic approaches and essential vaccines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-16T10:59:19.415Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x55r5h6j4g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x55r5h6j4g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kyle","last_name":"Buchan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g5trm4zx6r","title":"Anthrax ET activates Rac1 and RTK signaling to induce F-actin reorganization and endothelial permeability , Jain et al, 2025","description":"Anthrax Edema Toxin induces F-actin reorganization and edema in human Brain endothelial cells (HBMECs) in mouse footpads. Figure 1A: raw confocal images of F-actin stains (Phalloidin 488) of HBMECs used for the analyses presented in Figure 1B. Figure 1B: This data presents quantifications of F-actin fibers in untreated cells (UT), and in cells treated with ET, a mutant form of ET (ETmut) that cannot produce cAMP, and PA, the receptor-binding component of ET (ET = EF +PA). We find that ET causes a strong loss of actin stress fibers. Interestingly, ETmut and PA also induce a loss of actin stress fibers, but this phenotype is much weaker than for ET, indicating that cAMP production by EF is the main factor triggering F-actin reorganization, but that cAMP-independent effects are also at play. Figure 2C-Phalloidin: F-actin Phalloidin stains, showing Untreated HBMECs, cells treated cells with the Rac1 inhibitor NSC23766, ET-treated cells, and ET-treated cells with the Rac1 inhibitor NSC23766. Figure 2C: quantitative analysis of phalloidin stains. Rac1 inhibitor NSC23766 alleviates F-actin fiber loss by ET, indicating that Rac1 mediates ET effects on F-actin. Figure 6B-C: MEK inhibitor AS-703026, PI3K inhibitor GDC-0941 and Rac1 inhibitor NSC23766, prevent ET-induced foot pad edema in mice.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-20T22:33:52.266Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g5trm4zx6r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g5trm4zx6r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Annabel","last_name":"Guichard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ethan","last_name":"Bier","orcid":"0000-0002-2892-3005"},{"first_name":"Prashant","last_name":"Jain","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mahtab","last_name":"Moayeri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saluja","last_name":"Kaduswal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Margot","last_name":"Mel de Fontenay","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ian","last_name":"Rousseau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Steve","last_name":"Leppla","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cellular Toxicology","Actin","Endothelial Cell","Edema","Anthrax"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mtbgnz7yk5","title":"Ovarian Cancer Dataset - Cell Reports - Gonzalez et al 2021","description":"CyTOF dataset of Gonzalez et al., Cell Reports 2021\n\n1. NK and T cell infiltrates CyTOF files (CD45+CD66b- gated FCS files) for the 17 ovarian tumors from which we obtained correlations with a dl-NK cell phenotype.\n2. Tumor cells CyTOF files (CD45-CD31-FAP- gated FCS files) for the 12 tumors measuring the NK receptor ligands.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-31T09:02:09.453Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mtbgnz7yk5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mtbgnz7yk5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Veronica D.","last_name":"Gonzalez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying-Wen","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Antonio","last_name":"Delgado-Gonzalez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wendy J.","last_name":"Fantl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ovarian Cancer","Immune Response","T Cell","Natural Killer Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hrbmtfv754","title":"Programmed secretion arrest and receptor-triggered toxin export during antibacterial contact-dependent growth inhibition. Ruhe et al.","description":"Data for \"Programmed secretion arrest and receptor-triggered toxin export during antibacterial contact-dependent growth inhibition (CDI)\"\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-18T19:01:43.31Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hrbmtfv754","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hrbmtfv754"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christoper","last_name":"Hayes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dkpxj9ckkv","title":"Frey OR10A6 Data","description":"Data file for Frey T, Kwadha CA, Haag F, Pelletier J, Wallin EA, Holgersson E, Hedenström E, Bohman B, Bengtsson M, Becher PG, Krautwurst D, Witzgall P. 2022. The human odorant receptor OR10A6 is tuned to the pheromone of the commensal fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster. iScience (in press)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-30T15:21:43.616Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dkpxj9ckkv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dkpxj9ckkv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"peter","last_name":"witzgall","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Communication","Chemical Ecology","Drosophila","Olfactory Receptor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x83f4kkr9v","title":"Characterization of immune cells in oral tissues of non-human primates","description":"In this study, we assess immune cell populations across non-human primate (NHP), Macaca nemestrina oral tissues. Specifically, we assess tissue biopsies (Bx, Unbr) and cytobrushed (CB, Cybr) samples taken from buccal, sublingual, and lingual tonsil tissues. We also assess immune populations across sex and age differences. All samples are measured using flow cytometry to quantify relative cell frequencies as well as normalized cell quantities per tissue mass, or brushing, to assess the overall density of immune cell populations. Immune cells are determined from live, CD45+ leukocytes. Of these leukocytes, we identify CD45+CD3+ T-cells, CD45+CD3-CD20+ B-cells, and CD45+CD3-CD20-CD11c+ antigen-presenting cells (APCs). Finally, we assess subpopulations of T-cells and APCs. T-cells are further delineated as CD45+CD3+CD4+ or CD45+CD3+CD8+. APCs are separated as CD45+CD20-CD11c+CD1a+ Langerhans Cells (LCs) or CD45+CD20-CD11c+CD11b+ submucosal dendritic cells (SMDCs). Considering that the NHP model better represents the human immune system than mouse models, we believe these data can provide important information for understanding oral health. Further, these data can inform the optimal location and delivery strategies for novel oral mucosal vaccination methods. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-25T15:46:55.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x83f4kkr9v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x83f4kkr9v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jamie","last_name":"Hernandez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jaehyung","last_name":"Park","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Hughes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Florian","last_name":"Hladik","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kim","last_name":"Woodrow","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cells of the Immune System","Flow Cytometry","Antigen-Presenting Cell","Leukocyte","Mucosal Immunity","T Cell","Primate Cell","Mucosal Vaccine","Buccal Mucosa","Langerhans Cell","Sublingual Route","Nonhuman Primate","Mucosal Tissue"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g5fyhzt8mk","title":"Extracellular Staphylococcus aureus USA 300 Persister Cell Awakening Factor","description":"This is the raw data for experiments that identified and partially characterized an extracellular factor that awakens Staphylococcus aureus USA300 persister cells called staphylococcal awakening factor (SAF).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-03T07:33:05.592Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g5fyhzt8mk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g5fyhzt8mk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Neal","last_name":"Chamberlain","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medical Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4wpb5rjn76","title":"MELANOCORTIN RECEPTOR ACCESSORY PROTEIN 2-INDUCED ADRENOCORTICOTROPIC HORMONE RESPONSE OF HUMAN MELANOCORTIN 4 RECEPTOR","description":"Human (h) and zebrafish (zf) melanocortin receptor 4 (MC4R) activation data.\nOur data shows how both h and zfMC4R increase the sensitivity to the adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) when interacting with the melanocortin receptor two accessory protein type 2 (MRAP2). Therefore, MC4R becomes an ACTH receptor in the presence of MRAP2 thus opening new gates to the stress effects mediated by ACTH. This MRAP2-induced ACTH sensitivity of MC4R is evolutionary conserved (zebrafish-human) thus suggesting a physiological role for the MC4R/MRAP2 interaction  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-11-21T17:39:56.594Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4wpb5rjn76","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4wpb5rjn76"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lucia","last_name":"Soletto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sergio ","last_name":"Hernández Balfagó","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ana","last_name":"Rocha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gunnar ","last_name":"Kleinau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patrick","last_name":"Scheerer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose Miguel","last_name":"Cerda-Reverter","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5s4bs476zz","title":"Characterization and function of progesterone receptor membrane component 2 (PGRMC2) in the human endometrium - Annex 1.","description":"Supplementary material of the Ph.D. thesis entitled “Characterization and function of progesterone receptor membrane component 2 (PGRMC2) in the human endometrium”, presented by Yassmin Medina Laver, under the direction of Dr. Francisco Domínguez Hernández and Dr. José Bellver Pradas.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-12-16T12:52:07.455Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5s4bs476zz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5s4bs476zz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yassmin","last_name":"Medina Laver","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0097mvx21","name":"Generalitat Valenciana","grant_id":"ACIF/2019/262"}],"categories":["Proteomics","Biological Process","In Vitro Fertilization","RNA Sequencing","Infertility","Endometrium","Functional Analysis","Stromal Cell","Progesterone Receptor","Expression Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133525,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":156,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133525,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132996,"restricted":68,"embargoed":461},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2240},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":663},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":471},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":402},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":380},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":326},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":274},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":220}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130891},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1907},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":180},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":132},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":70},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":506},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":358},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":